XXXX. Zelles Terms & Conditions Sender Responsibility and Finality of Transfers Under the Zelle Network User Service Agreement ( available at zellepay.com/legal/user-service-agreement ), Zelle explicitly informs users that : Once you authorize a payment to be sent, you may not be able to cancel it if the recipient is already enrolled with Zelle.You are solely responsible for ensuring the accuracy of the information you enter for each transaction, including the recipients name, U.S. mobile phone number, or email address. 
Furthermore, Zelles terms emphasize that : Zelle is intended to be used with people you know and trust. Neither Zelle nor the participating financial institutions will be liable for losses arising from your use of the service with individuals you do not know or trust. 
This means that once the sender confirmed and authorized the payment, Zelle processed the transaction immediately and irrevocably, relying on the senders own authentication and confirmation. 
The system requires multiple confirmation steps before a payment is sent, including : * Verifying the recipients name, phone, or email; * Reviewing a final confirmation screen showing the recipients details and amount; * Acknowledging the disclaimer that Zelle payments are final once sent to enrolled users. 
Therefore, the sender can not later report the transaction as unauthorized merely because they regret sending it or wish to reclaim the funds. Doing so constitutes a false and fraudulent use of the Zelle dispute process. 

XXXX. Legal Framework and Consumer Protection Standards Under the Electronic Fund Transfer Act ( XXXX ), XXXX XXXX. XXXX et seq., and Regulation XXXX ( XXXX CFR Part XXXX ), financial institutions are required to investigate and resolve unauthorized electronic fund transfers. However, an unauthorized transfer is defined narrowly as : An electronic fund transfer from a consumers account initiated by a person other than the consumer without actual authority to initiate such transfer and from which the consumer receives no benefit. 12 CFR 1005.2 ( m ) In this case : * The sender personally authorized, confirmed, and executed the transfer; * The sender benefited from the system functioning exactly as designed ; * Therefore, the transfer does not meet the definition of unauthorized under the law.

Consequently, any claim of unauthorized made by the sender is invalid under Regulation E, and I, as the recipient, can not be held liable for their false claim.

